.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-RESTORE-MACHINE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-restore-machine
.Nd rebuild a machine on an empty disk from its replicated ZFS archive, and leave it bootable
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -archive-host Ar host
.Fl -archive Ar dataset
.Fl -disk Ar device
.Fl -yes | -dry-run
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Fl -archive-url Ar url
.Fl -disk Ar device
.Fl -yes | -dry-run
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Replication copies a machine's pool to another host.
.Nm
is the way back: run from the netbooted live environment on the machine
that is to become the old one, it repartitions a disk, receives the
archive onto it, and repairs what a receive alone leaves broken, so the
machine boots.
.Pp
In order, it:
.Bl -enum
.It
Checks the disk.
It must be a whole disk, or a loop device, that this host is not using:
not mounted, not swap, not a device of an imported pool, not attached to a
running virtual machine \(em compared after resolving links, so a by-id
name cannot slip past.
It checks that the archive answers and holds datasets.
.It
Partitions the disk: an EFI system partition first, then the rest for ZFS.
.It
Creates the pool, with its root under
.Pa /mnt/restore ,
and receives the archive into it with
.Xr syncoid 8 ,
one dataset at a time, each sent decrypted by the archive host and
encrypted again, if at all, by the new pool's own policy.
From a URL it receives one
.Ql zfs send -R
stream.
.It
Marks the first
.Pa */ROOT/*
dataset as the boot environment and the pool's
.Cm bootfs .
.It
Repairs what the old machine's files get wrong on new hardware:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
pool import by scanning instead of by cache file, whose device paths are
the old machine's, and a new initramfs for the newest kernel on the pool;
.It
the original host id, from the manifest;
.It
.Pa /etc/fstab ,
repointed at the new EFI partition;
.It
NetworkManager connections pinned to the old machine's MAC addresses, which
are unpinned.
.El
.It
Puts a bootloader in the EFI partition: the old one, from an archive made
by
.Xr kldload-boot-manifest 8 ,
or else
.Xr grub2-install 8 .
.It
Checks the outcome \(em kernel, initramfs,
.Cm bootfs ,
.Pa fstab ,
an EFI binary, scan import, the host id \(em and exits 1 if any is missing.
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl -dry-run
it runs the checks in step 1 and prints what it would do after them,
changing nothing.
It needs root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -archive-host Ar host
The host holding the archive; it does the sending, over
.Xr ssh 1 .
.Ql local
when this is that host.
.It Fl -archive Ar dataset
The archived tree on that host, such as
.Ql rpool/backup/fiend .
.It Fl -archive-url Ar url
Instead of the two above: a
.Ql zfs send
stream over HTTP, as
.Xr kldload-netboot-server 8
serves a golden.
.It Fl -disk Ar device
The disk to repartition.
.It Fl -pool Ar name
The pool to create; default
.Ql rpool ,
or the manifest's.
There must be no pool of that name imported here.
.It Fl -hostname Ar name
Written to the restored
.Pa /etc/hostname .
.It Fl -esp-size Ar size
The EFI partition's size; default 1GiB, or the manifest's.
.It Fl -ssh-key Ar path
The key for reaching the archive host.
.It Fl -manifest Ar file
The boot manifest from
.Xr kldload-boot-manifest 8 :
pool name, EFI partition size, host name, host id and the EFI archive, so
the rebuild matches the machine instead of guessing.
.It Fl -esp-archive Ar file
A tar of the old EFI partition, unpacked into the new one.
.It Fl -yes
Required to change the disk.
.It Fl -dry-run
Check, then print the plan; change nothing.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /mnt/restore
Where the new pool is mounted while it is repaired.
.It Pa etc/fstab.pre-restore , Pa *.nmconnection.pre-restore
The restored machine's originals, kept beside the edited copies.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Restored and verified, or the dry run finished.
.It 1
A step failed, or the verification did.
.It 2
A usage error, or the disk or archive was refused.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, whose root pool is on
.Pa nvme0n1p2
and a second pool on
.Pa nvme1n1p1 ,
and which holds another machine's archive in
.Ql rpool/backup/fiend .
.Ss The plan, on a disk nothing uses
A 64 MB throwaway volume, destroyed afterwards:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166 --pool rtest --dry-run
archive: local:rpool/backup/fiend — 82 datasets
partitioning /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166 (ESP 1GiB, remainder ZFS)
  would run: sgdisk --zap-all /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166
  would run: sgdisk -n1:0:+1GiB -t1:EF00 -c1:EFI System /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166
  would run: sgdisk -n2:0:0 -t2:BF00 -c2:rtest /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166
  would run: mkfs.vfat -F32 -n EFI /dev/zvol/rpool/rmtest-3916166-part1
\&...
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
The dry run does not know the volume is too small for the partition it
plans.
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/nvme0n1 --pool rtest --dry-run
FATAL: /dev/nvme0n1 (/dev/nvme0n1) or a partition of it is mounted or is swap on this host — refusing
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-eui.002538b71150e07b --pool rtest --dry-run
FATAL: /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-eui.002538b71150e07b (/dev/nvme0n1) or a partition of it is mounted or is swap on this host — refusing
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/nvme0n1p2 --pool rtest --dry-run
FATAL: /dev/nvme0n1p2 (/dev/nvme0n1p2) is a part, not a whole disk
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/nvme1n1 --dry-run
FATAL: /dev/nvme1n1 (/dev/nvme1n1p1) is a device of a pool imported on this host — refusing
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk /dev/nvme1n1
FATAL: --yes is required: /dev/nvme1n1 will be repartitioned
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host local --archive rpool/backup/fiend --disk '' --dry-run
FATAL: --disk needs a value
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Ss A full restore
Not run for this page.
From the live environment on the replacement machine, with the archive on
the backup host and the manifest and EFI archive copied from it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-restore-machine --archive-host backup --archive rpool/backup/fiend \e
      --disk /dev/nvme0n1 --manifest fiend.manifest.json --yes
# ends "OK: rpool restored from backup:rpool/backup/fiend and verified.
# Reboot into it." or with the checks that failed.
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr kldload-autoinstall 8 ,
.Xr kldload-backup-restore 8 ,
.Xr kldload-boot-manifest 8 ,
.Xr kldload-netboot-server 8 ,
.Xr syncoid 8 ,
.Xr zfs-receive 8
.Sh HISTORY
Written after fiend was restored by hand on 2026-09-09: the pool came back
perfect and the machine still did not boot, first because the initramfs
imported by the old machine's device paths, then because
.Pa /etc/fstab
named an EFI partition that no longer existed.
Each repair in step 5 is one of those.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: the check that the disk was not in use,
which the tool's own header described, did not exist \(em pointed at the
disk holding the running root pool, it planned to wipe it, and with
.Fl -yes
would have.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
A running virtual machine's disk is refused, but that check has not been
seen to fire: no machine was running where it was tested.
.Pp
Secure Boot keys are not restored; a machine that had Secure Boot on needs
someone at its console to enrol the key again, and the manifest says so.
.Pp
The initramfs is built with
.Xr dracut 8 ,
so the archive must be of a system that has it.
.Pp
.Xr kldload-autoinstall 8
runs this with
.Fl -yes
on the disk named on the kernel command line.
